WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.960
Stop mowing a field in the Virginia Piedmont.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.020
Nobody plants anything.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.480
Seeds blow in or wait in the soil.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.660
Within a few years, the field can fill with broomsedge, blackberry and small pines.

5
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.480
After a century or more, oaks and hickories can take over.

6
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.280
What happens to a field after people or fire leave it alone?

7
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:27.133
This change has a name: ecological succession.

8
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:32.473
A community is all the populations of different species living in one area.

9
00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:37.533
Succession is the change, over years to centuries, in which species live there.

10
00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:40.573
The change often follows a general pattern.

11
00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:43.733
Certain plants tend to come early, others late.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:47.217
Succession often starts after a disturbance.

13
00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:53.057
A disturbance is an event, like a fire, flood or plowing, that removes much of a

14
00:00:53.057 --> 00:00:56.917
community. If the soil stays, it is secondary succession.

15
00:00:57.897 --> 00:00:59.337
On bare rock there is no soil.

16
00:01:00.057 --> 00:01:04.237
That is primary succession, and lichens and mosses usually arrive first.

17
00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:08.477
Secondary succession usually reaches forest much faster.

18
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.160
Here is the usual path published for old Piedmont fields, just south of Virginia.

19
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.460
In the first years, weeds such as horseweed and asters grow fast in full sun.

20
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.400
By about the third year, broomsedge, a tall grass, often covers the field.

21
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.780
Pines grow fast in full sun.

22
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.780
Within a few decades, the field becomes a young pine forest.

23
00:01:31.783 --> 00:01:34.203
Each stage changes the place it grows in.

24
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:39.843
Abiotic factors are the nonliving parts of an ecosystem, such as light and soil.

25
00:01:40.723 --> 00:01:43.303
A closed pine canopy blocks much of the sunlight.

26
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:46.043
Pine seedlings grow poorly in that shade.

27
00:01:47.003 --> 00:01:48.643
Young hardwoods cope with shade better.

28
00:01:49.603 --> 00:01:53.723
Oaks and hickories can survive some shade, but they grow best where a gap lets in

29
00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:57.383
light. As the old pines die, the hardwoods fill the gaps.

30
00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:00.457
So why do plants arrive in this order?

31
00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:05.417
Each stage changes light and soil, and the plants that cope best with the new

32
00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:06.517
conditions take over.

33
00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:11.757
In most of Virginia, the late stage has been called an oak-hickory climax community,

34
00:02:12.337 --> 00:02:13.337
a hardwood forest.

35
00:02:14.097 --> 00:02:18.197
But fire, storms and people reset patches, so the path and timing vary.

36
00:02:19.157 --> 00:02:22.697
Each stage changes the place, and the plants that cope best take over.
